I fixed it: a scoped style whose `@media` (or `@container`, or any other at-rule) sits inside a rule with nested selectors now gets the data attribute. The existing `compileStyle` tests all pass. I didn't add a new test.

**The cause:** When a scoped rule has nested child rules, the compiler moves the rule's own declarations into an `& { ... }` block and adds the scope attribute there. It did that only for declarations directly inside the rule. Declarations directly inside a nested at-rule were left alone, so they ended up with less specificity than the root declarations.

**The fix**, in `packages/compiler-sfc/src/style/pluginScoped.ts`: I moved the wrapping logic into a small helper, `extractAndWrapNodes`. It now runs on the rule itself and on each at-rule nested directly inside it. Deep (`:deep`) selectors are still skipped, as before.

I checked it with this input:

```css
.foo { background-color: red; .bar {} @media only screen and (max-width: 800px) { background-color: green; .baz{color:red} } }
```

It now compiles to:

```css
.foo {
&[data-v-test] { background-color: red
}
.bar[data-v-test] {}
@media only screen and (max-width: 800px) {
&[data-v-test] { background-color: green
}
.baz[data-v-test]{color:red}
}
}
```

This matches what the issue expects; the line breaks are just how the existing code prints.

Two limits:
- Only at-rules nested directly inside the rule are handled. An at-rule inside another at-rule (say `@supports` inside `@media`) still won't get its declarations wrapped.
- Comments next to the declarations stay where they are rather than moving into the `&` block, which is how the code already behaved.

